      "id": "1231416966722301",
      "score": 5,
      "review_title": "Really good.",
      "review_description": "Clouds Gorilla Horror is genuinely one of the most enjoyable VR horror experiences I have played. From the moment you enter the game, it creates an atmosphere that feels unsettling, mysterious, and immersive. The environment design, lighting, sounds, and overall presentation work together extremely well to make you feel like you are actually inside a horror world rather than just playing a game.\n\nOne of the biggest strengths of Clouds Gorilla Horror is how it uses the Gorilla Tag-style movement system in a horror setting. The movement makes every chase, escape, and exploration moment feel much more intense because you are physically moving, climbing, and trying to survive rather than just pressing buttons. It adds a completely different level of immersion that you do not get in normal horror games.\n\nThe attention to detail throughout the game is impressive. The maps feel carefully designed, the atmosphere keeps you on edge, and the unexpected moments make exploring exciting. The horror elements are balanced really well, creating tension without relying only on cheap jumpscares. The game does a great job at making you feel like something could happen at any moment.\n\nI also really appreciate the creativity behind the game. It feels like a project made with passion, with its own identity and ideas instead of just copying other VR horror games. The developers clearly put effort into making an experience that is fun, memorable, and enjoyable with friends or even alone.\n\nOverall, Clouds Gorilla Horror is an amazing VR horror game that deserves much more attention. Whether you enjoy Gorilla Tag movement, horror games, or just looking for something fun and immersive in VR, this game is definitely worth trying. The atmosphere, gameplay, and creativity make it an easy 5-star experience. I’m excited to see what the developers add in the future! ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐",
      "date": 1785075805,
      "review_helpful_count": 11,
      "author_display_name": "lix.810419",
      "author_alias": "lix.810419",
      "developer_response": null
